---
/**
 * Will it run on my hosting.
 *
 * This is the page that prevents refunds, so it is deliberately blunt about PHP 8.4
 * rather than burying it. The figures come from docs-site install/requirements.
 */
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base
	title="Requirements — Ultimate Client Manager"
	description="What your hosting account needs before you buy: PHP 8.4, MySQL or MariaDB, Apache with mod_rewrite, and about 120 MB of disk."
	current="/requirements/"
>
	<section class="hero">
		<div class="wrap narrow">
			<p class="kicker">Before you buy</p>
			<h1>Will it run on your hosting?</h1>
			<p class="lead">
				Almost certainly, with one thing to check first. Most shared hosting accounts sold
				today meet all of this — the PHP version is the one that catches people out.
			</p>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section>
		<div class="wrap">
			<div class="scroller">
				<table class="compare">
					<thead>
						<tr>
							<th scope="col">You need</th>
							<th scope="col">Version</th>
						</tr>
					</thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr>
							<th scope="row">PHP</th>
							<td><strong>8.4 or newer.</strong> 8.4 and 8.5 are both tested on every change.</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<th scope="row">MySQL or MariaDB</th>
							<td>MySQL 8.0, or MariaDB 10.5 or newer.</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<th scope="row">Web server</th>
							<td>Apache with <code>mod_rewrite</code>. nginx works with caveats.</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<th scope="row">Disk space</th>
							<td>About 120 MB, plus whatever your uploaded files come to.</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<th scope="row">PHP extensions</th>
							<td>
								<code>mysqli</code>, <code>simplexml</code> and <code>curl</code> are
								required. <code>gd</code>, <code>zip</code>, <code>imap</code>,
								<code>openssl</code>, <code>mbstring</code> and <code>fileinfo</code>
								each switch on a particular feature.
							</td>
						</tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</div>

			<div class="note narrow">
				<p>
					<strong>PHP 8.4 is a hard requirement.</strong> The application checks the version
					before it does anything else and stops with a message if it is too old. There is
					no way to override it. If your host still offers only PHP 7.x or 8.0 to 8.3, ask
					them to move your account — most control panels have a PHP version selector that
					does it in a few seconds.
				</p>
			</div>

			<div class="narrow">
				<h2>How to check</h2>
				<p>
					Your hosting control panel will have a PHP version selector, usually called
					<em>PHP Selector</em>, <em>MultiPHP Manager</em> or <em>Select PHP Version</em>.
					The same screen normally lists the extensions, which is where to confirm
					<code>curl</code> and <code>simplexml</code>.
				</p>
				<p>
					You also need an empty MySQL database and a user with full rights on it. The
					installer creates the tables, indexes and foreign keys, so granting all
					privileges on that one database is the usual way to do it.
				</p>

				<h2>If you are on nginx</h2>
				<p>
					It works, with caveats that matter: the rules that deny direct web access to
					uploaded files are written as Apache <code>.htaccess</code> files, and nginx does
					not read those. The
					<a href="/docs/install/requirements/">full requirements page</a> gives the
					configuration you need instead. Do not skip it — without it, anything a user
					uploads can be fetched off your server by anyone who guesses the address.
				</p>

				<h2>Upgrading an existing installation</h2>
				<p>
					This release raises the minimum PHP version and changes where database settings
					are stored, so it is not a files-over-the-top upgrade from 2.x. Read
					<a href="/docs/install/upgrading/">the upgrade guide</a> before you copy anything
					over a working site.
				</p>

				<div class="actions">
					<a class="button quiet" href="/docs/install/requirements/">The full requirements page</a>
					<a class="button" href="https://codecanyon.net/item/freelance-project-invoice-crm/2621629">
						Buy on CodeCanyon
					</a>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>
</Base>
